<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Orthogonal Polynomial Background</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Orthogonal polynomial (OP) sequences provide a classical and well-studied family of bases for representing functions with respect to a weighted inner product. Given a probability density (or more generally, a positive measure) <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(x)</annotation></semantics></math> on a domain <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, an orthonormal polynomial sequence <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{P_{n}(x)\}_{n=0}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{P_{n}(x)\}_{n=0}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence of degree-<math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> polynomials satisfying</p>
<table id="A1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle P_{m},P_{n}\rangle_{\omega}\triangleq\int_{\mathcal{X}}P_{m}(x)P_{n}(x)\omega(x)\mathrm{d}x=\delta_{mn}\;." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">≜</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle P_{m},P_{n}\rangle_{\omega}\triangleq\int_{\mathcal{X}}P_{m}(x)P_{n}(x)\omega(x)\mathrm{d}x=\delta_{mn}\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Any square-integrable function with respect to <math id="A1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> can be expanded in this basis, with coefficients given by inner products against the corresponding polynomials.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A fundamental structural property of OP sequences is that they satisfy a three-term recurrence relation. Favard’s theorem states that a sequence of polynomials <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{P_{n}(x)\}_{n=0}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{P_{n}(x)\}_{n=0}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> is an orthogonal polynomial sequence with respect to some positive measure on the real line if and only if it satisfies a recurrence of the form</p>
<table id="A1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xP_{n}(x)=a_{n+1}P_{n+1}(x)+b_{n}P_{n}(x)+a_{n}P_{n-1}(x)\;,\quad a_{n}&gt;0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xP_{n}(x)=a_{n+1}P_{n+1}(x)+b_{n}P_{n}(x)+a_{n}P_{n-1}(x)\;,\quad a_{n}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, with appropriate initialization. This recurrence relation underlies the structured linear operators that appear in HiPPO dynamics that enable their computational efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Different choices of the measure <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> induce different orthogonal polynomial families and corresponding notions of approximation error. Classical families such as Legendre, Laguerre, and Chebyshev polynomials correspond to different weighting schemes and lead to distinct memory behaviors when used within HiPPO-style systems. In this work, we focus on a specific orthogonal polynomial family that admits efficient structured dynamics and well-conditioned numerical behavior: the Legendre polynomials. Below, we define the Legendre polynomials, while explicit forms of the HiPPO systems used in this paper are listed in Table <a href="#A2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Appendix B HiPPO Systems Table ‣ HiPPO Zoo: Explicit Memory Mechanisms for Interpretable State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. We refer the reader to the survey of Totik and the book of Chihara for more information on orthogonal polynomials <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Totik, 2005</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chihara, 1978</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Orthonormal Legendre polynomials on <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the standard Legendre polynomials on <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, defined by the recurrence</p>
<table id="A1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n+1)P_{n+1}(x)=(2n+1)xP_{n}(x)-nP_{n-1}(x),\qquad P_{0}(x)=1,\;P_{1}(x)=x," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n+1)P_{n+1}(x)=(2n+1)xP_{n}(x)-nP_{n-1}(x),\qquad P_{0}(x)=1,\;P_{1}(x)=x,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and orthogonal with respect to the uniform measure on <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Throughout this paper we use a shifted and rescaled version that is orthonormal on <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}(s)\;\triangleq\;\sqrt{2n+1}\,(-1)^{n}\,P_{n}(2s-1),\qquad s\in[0,1]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≜</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}(s)\;\triangleq\;\sqrt{2n+1}\,(-1)^{n}\,P_{n}(2s-1),\qquad s\in[0,1].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">These polynomials satisfy</p>
<table id="A1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{0}^{1}L_{n}(s)\,L_{m}(s)\,\mathrm{d}s=\delta_{nm}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{0}^{1}L_{n}(s)\,L_{m}(s)\,\mathrm{d}s=\delta_{nm},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">and are oriented so that <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the present and <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the furthest point in the represented past.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>HiPPO Systems Table</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For completeness, Table <a href="#A2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Appendix B HiPPO Systems Table ‣ HiPPO Zoo: Explicit Memory Mechanisms for Interpretable State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the continuous-time HiPPO systems used throughout the paper. We focus on two Legendre-based constructions that differ only in their choice of weighting measure over the past. Both systems admit closed-form expressions for the HiPPO dynamics matrices <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,\mathbf{b})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,\mathbf{b})</annotation></semantics></math> and an orthonormal polynomial basis, enabling stable and efficient continuous-time implementations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A2.T1.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A2.T1.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T1.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r"></th>
<th id="A2.T1.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><math id="A2.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A2.T1.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><math id="A2.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A2.T1.3.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><math id="A2.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{n}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A2.T1.3.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><math id="A2.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{nk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{nk}</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A2.T1.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T1.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 5.16663pt;"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:9.5pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span>
Leg-T</th>
<td id="A2.T1.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 5.16663pt;"><math id="A2.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.T1.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 5.16663pt;"><math id="A2.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.T1.3.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 5.16663pt;"><math id="A2.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2n+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.T1.3.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 5.16663pt;"><math id="A2.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}b_{n}b_{k}(-1)^{n-k+1},&amp;n&gt;k\\
b_{n}b_{k},&amp;n=k\\
0,&amp;n&lt;k\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}b_{n}b_{k}(-1)^{n-k+1},&amp;n&gt;k\\
b_{n}b_{k},&amp;n=k\\
0,&amp;n&lt;k\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T1.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T1.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">Leg-S</th>
<td id="A2.T1.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(-\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(-\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.T1.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}(e^{-\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}(e^{-\tau})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.T1.3.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2n+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.T1.3.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}0,&amp;n&lt;k\\
-b_{n}b_{k}\dfrac{n+1}{2n+1},&amp;n=k\\
-b_{n}b_{k},&amp;n&gt;k\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}0,&amp;n&lt;k\\
-b_{n}b_{k}\dfrac{n+1}{2n+1},&amp;n=k\\
-b_{n}b_{k},&amp;n&gt;k\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="A2.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Orthogonal Polynomial HiPPO systems used in this paper. Leg-T corresponds to the truncated Legendre HiPPO system defined on a finite window, while Leg-S corresponds to the exponentially weighted (sliding) Legendre HiPPO system.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Experimental Details</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Quadratic Volterra Example</h3>

<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task and Data</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate Volterra HiPPO on the nonlinear benchmark system of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wray and Green (1994)</a></cite>, described below, which admits a purely second-order Volterra representation. The input <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is a band-limited white noise signal generated with sampling step <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t=10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t=10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math> and cutoff frequency <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>. The target output <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is produced by applying the Wray-Green system to the input stream, yielding a sequence <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(f_{t},y_{t})\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(f_{t},y_{t})\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=10^{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=10^{7}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The nonlinear benchmark introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wray and Green (1994)</a></cite> is a purely second-order Volterra system with a separable quadratic kernel. Let <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the input and define the impulse response</p>
<table id="A3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\tau)=\frac{a}{m}e^{-k\tau}\sin(m\tau)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\tau)=\frac{a}{m}e^{-k\tau}\sin(m\tau),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,m,k&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,m,k&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed constants. The output is given by a quadratic convolution</p>
<table id="A3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y(t)=\int_{0}^{\tau_{\max}}\int_{0}^{\tau_{\max}}h_{2}(\tau_{1},\tau_{2})\,f(t-\tau_{1})\,f(t-\tau_{2})\,d\tau_{1}d\tau_{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y(t)=\int_{0}^{\tau_{\max}}\int_{0}^{\tau_{\max}}h_{2}(\tau_{1},\tau_{2})\,f(t-\tau_{1})\,f(t-\tau_{2})\,d\tau_{1}d\tau_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">with separable kernel</p>
<table id="A3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{2}(\tau_{1},\tau_{2})=\alpha\,\mu(\tau_{1})\,\mu(\tau_{2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{2}(\tau_{1},\tau_{2})=\alpha\,\mu(\tau_{1})\,\mu(\tau_{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is a scaling constant. Equivalently, letting</p>
<table id="A3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z(t)=\int_{0}^{\tau_{\max}}\mu(\tau)\,f(t-\tau)\,d\tau," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z(t)=\int_{0}^{\tau_{\max}}\mu(\tau)\,f(t-\tau)\,d\tau,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">the system reduces to <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y(t)=\alpha\,z(t)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y(t)=\alpha\,z(t)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, the Wray-Green system has no linear term and admits an exact second-order Volterra representation. In our implementation, <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0.3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0.08" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.08</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0.08</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\max}=50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\max}=50</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.004" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.004</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.004</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">HiPPO Memory</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We maintain a HiPPO memory state <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=64</annotation></semantics></math> using a scaled Legendre HiPPO system (Leg-S). Let <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,\mathbf{b})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,\mathbf{b})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the continuous-time HiPPO parameters for Leg-S. We rescale the system by a constant timescale factor <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.375" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.375</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.375</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\leftarrow A/\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\leftarrow A/\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}\leftarrow\mathbf{b}/\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}\leftarrow\mathbf{b}/\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. We then discretize the dynamics with zero-order hold:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{d}=\exp(\Delta tA)\;,\quad\mathbf{b}_{d}=A^{-1}(A_{d}-I)\mathbf{b}\;," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐛</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.280em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{d}=\exp(\Delta tA)\;,\quad\mathbf{b}_{d}=A^{-1}(A_{d}-I)\mathbf{b}\;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and evolve the state online as <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{t+1}=A_{d}\mathbf{s}_{t}+\mathbf{b}_{d}f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{t+1}=A_{d}\mathbf{s}_{t}+\mathbf{b}_{d}f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. During training, the HiPPO dynamics are fixed. Only the readout parameters are learned.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Readouts (linear, quadratic, and MLP)</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare three predictors trained online from the shared state <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ul id="A3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Linear Volterra readout:</span> <math id="A3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{t}=b+\mathbf{w}_{1}^{\top}\mathbf{s}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{t}=b+\mathbf{w}_{1}^{\top}\mathbf{s}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quadratic Volterra readout:</span> <math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{t}=b+\mathbf{w}_{1}^{\top}\mathbf{s}_{t}+\mathbf{s}_{t}^{\top}W_{2}\mathbf{s}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{t}=b+\mathbf{w}_{1}^{\top}\mathbf{s}_{t}+\mathbf{s}_{t}^{\top}W_{2}\mathbf{s}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> is an unconstrained quadratic coefficient matrix.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MLP readout:</span> a single-hidden-layer network with <math id="A3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{t}=b_{0}+W_{o}\mathrm{tanh}(W_{h}\mathbf{s}_{t}+b_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>tanh</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{t}=b_{0}+W_{o}\mathrm{tanh}(W_{h}\mathbf{s}_{t}+b_{h})</annotation></semantics></math> with hidden width <math id="A3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training Protocol</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All three predictors are trained by online stochastic gradient descent on the squared error <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{y}_{t}-y_{t})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{y}_{t}-y_{t})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. For the linear and quadratic readouts we use SGD with a learning rate <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math> and for the MLP we use SGD with a learning rate <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We report cumulative error <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{s=1}^{t}(\hat{y}_{s}-y_{s})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{s=1}^{t}(\hat{y}_{s}-y_{s})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of time <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> on log-log axes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">True Kernel Visualization</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To visualize the ground-truth second-order Volterra kernel used in the experiment, we plot a discretized kernel <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{2}(\tau_{1},\tau_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{2}(\tau_{1},\tau_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1},\tau_{2}\in\{0,\dots,49\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>49</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1},\tau_{2}\in\{0,\dots,49\}</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{2}(\tau_{1},\tau_{2})\propto\mu(\tau_{1})\mu(\tau_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{2}(\tau_{1},\tau_{2})\propto\mu(\tau_{1})\mu(\tau_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\tau)=\frac{a}{m}e^{-k\tau}\sin(mt)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\tau)=\frac{a}{m}e^{-k\tau}\sin(mt)</annotation></semantics></math> and constants <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=2.0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0.3</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0.08" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.08</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0.08</annotation></semantics></math>. The result is scaled by <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> to match the Wray-Green system implementation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Inferred Kernel from the Quadratic Readout</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To interpret the learned quadratic coefficients <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in the time-lag domain, we reconstruct the implied second-order kernel on a grid <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\{0,\dots,49\}\Delta t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>49</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\{0,\dots,49\}\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{P_{n}\}_{n=0}^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{P_{n}\}_{n=0}^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Legendre HiPPO system. We form basis evaluations <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}(\tau/\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}(\tau/\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> and combine them with the learned coefficients and the induced lag-density <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> associated with the scaled timescale, <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(\tau)\propto\frac{\Delta t}{\alpha}\exp(-\tau/\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>α</mi></mfrac><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\tau)\propto\frac{\Delta t}{\alpha}\exp(-\tau/\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>, to obtain an inferred kernel of the form</p>
<table id="A3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{h}_{2}(\tau_{1},\tau_{2})\propto p(\tau_{1})p(\tau_{2})\sum_{i,j=0}^{N-1}(W_{2})_{ij}P_{i}(\tau_{1}/\alpha)P_{j}(\tau_{2}/\alpha)\;," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{h}_{2}(\tau_{1},\tau_{2})\propto p(\tau_{1})p(\tau_{2})\sum_{i,j=0}^{N-1}(W_{2})_{ij}P_{i}(\tau_{1}/\alpha)P_{j}(\tau_{2}/\alpha)\;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which is then visualized with a symmetric diverging colormap.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Selective Copying Experiment</h3>

<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task and Data</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate Salience HiPPO on an iterative selective copying task with vector-valued tokens (18 unique tokens: 1 <span id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Write</span> token, 1 uninformative token, and 16 informative tokens, all uniformly randomly drawn unit norm tokens in <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{32}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>32</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{32}</annotation></semantics></math>). Each episode has length <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=30</annotation></semantics></math> and consists of two phases. In the first phase, 10 informative tokens are randomly interleaved with 10 uninformative tokens. In the final 10 timesteps (the write phase), the special write token is presented at every timestep and the model is required to output the informative tokens in the order in which they were presented.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">HiPPO Memory</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The model maintains a multi-channel HiPPO memory <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}\times N}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{model}}=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{model}}=64</annotation></semantics></math> channels and <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=256</annotation></semantics></math> coefficients per channel, using the Leg-S HiPPO system. Concretely, this consists of <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{model}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{model}}</annotation></semantics></math> independent and identical HiPPO systems (one for each channel) with no cross-channel interactions. The continuous-time dynamics are discretized with a zero-order hold scheme. The HiPPO parameters <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,\mathbf{b})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,\mathbf{b})</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed throughout training, scaled by the episode length to match the task timescale.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Salience Parameterization</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The salience signal <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)\in(0,g_{\text{max}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)\in(0,g_{\text{max}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a learned scalar produced at each timestep. The current input token is first projected into the model dimension, yielding <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}^{\text{proj}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>proj</mtext></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}^{\text{proj}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In parallel, a low-dimensional summary of the current memory state is computed by applying a learned linear map to each channel of <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, passing through a <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{tanh}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>tanh</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{tanh}</annotation></semantics></math> nonlinearity, and averaging across channels. The concatenation of <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}^{\text{proj}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>proj</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}^{\text{proj}}</annotation></semantics></math> and this pooled memory summary is passed through a small MLP with a single hidden layer of width 128, softplus hidden activation, and a scaled sigmoid output activation to produce <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. This scalar modulates the effective integration rate of the HiPPO dynamics, yielding an input- and state-dependent deformation of the history measure.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training Protocol</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Models are trained online using truncated backpropagation through time (TBPTT). The concatenated episodes are processed sequentially and divided into chunks of length <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, the episode length. Gradients are computed within each chunk, and the HiPPO state is detached between chunks. All neural parameters (input projection, salience network, and readout) are optimized with AdamW using a learning rate of <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Interpretability Diagnostics</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For evaluation, we record the learned salience trace <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)</annotation></semantics></math> over an episode and compute the induced warped-time map <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(t)=\int_{0}^{t}g(s)\mathrm{d}s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(t)=\int_{0}^{t}g(s)\mathrm{d}s</annotation></semantics></math> under the assumption that <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is piecewise constant between discrete timesteps. Using this map, we visualize the induced history measures in real time and the effective linear functionals applied to history during the write phase. These diagnostics are used to generate the plots in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Selective Copying Experiment ‣ 3.2 Salience HiPPO ‣ 3 Methods and Results ‣ HiPPO Zoo: Explicit Memory Mechanisms for Interpretable State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, illustrating how selective copying is implemented through explicit reshaping of the history measure.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Associative Recall Experiment</h3>

<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task and Data</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate Associative Memory HiPPO on a synthetic associative recall task designed to isolate online key–value binding and retrieval. Each episode has even length <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=12</annotation></semantics></math> and consists of alternating tokens from two disjoint sets <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\{a_{1},\dots,a_{12}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\{a_{1},\dots,a_{12}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\{b_{1},\dots,b_{12}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\{b_{1},\dots,b_{12}\}</annotation></semantics></math> followed by a final <span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Write</span> token. Concretely, timesteps <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0,2,\dots,T-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0,2,\dots,T-2</annotation></semantics></math> present <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, timesteps <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,3,\dots,T-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,3,\dots,T-3</annotation></semantics></math> present <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, and <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=T-1</annotation></semantics></math> presents <span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Write</span>. The query token at time <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\!-\!2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\!-\!2</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> token that is guaranteed to have appeared previously among the <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> positions. The target output at time <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\!-\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\!-\!1</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> token that followed the most recent prior occurrence of this queried <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> token; outputs are zero at all other timesteps. Inputs and outputs are represented as normalized vectors in <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{24}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>24</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{24}</annotation></semantics></math> using a fixed random token table.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fixed-ZOH HiPPO Encoder</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We maintain a HiPPO state <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}\times n_{\text{hippo}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>hippo</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}\times n_{\text{hippo}}}</annotation></semantics></math> updated independently per channel, where <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{model}}=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{model}}=32</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{hippo}}=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>hippo</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{hippo}}=32</annotation></semantics></math>. The input <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{24}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>24</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{24}</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped to channels via a learned linear projection <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}=W_{\text{in}}x_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>model</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}=W_{\text{in}}x_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using a single precomputed zero-order-hold (ZOH) discretization of a Leg-T HiPPO scheme and a fixed timescale <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.0</annotation></semantics></math>, the recurrence is</p>
<table id="A3.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S_{t+1}[j]\;=\;A_{d}\,S_{t}[j]+b_{d}\,u_{t}[j],\qquad j=1,\dots,d_{\text{model}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t+1}[j]\;=\;A_{d}\,S_{t}[j]+b_{d}\,u_{t}[j],\qquad j=1,\dots,d_{\text{model}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}[j]\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{\text{hippo}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>hippo</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}[j]\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{\text{hippo}}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th channel state.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">OP Associative Memory State</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we maintain an associative memory bank</p>
<table id="A3.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}\times n_{\text{assoc}}}\;," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>assoc</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}\times n_{\text{assoc}}}\;,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{assoc}}=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>assoc</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{assoc}}=32</annotation></semantics></math>. Each row <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}[j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}[j]</annotation></semantics></math> represents the coefficients of a function <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{j}(\cdot;t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{j}(\cdot;t)</annotation></semantics></math> on a continuous address space <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> using an orthonormal Legendre basis <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{\mathrm{assoc}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>assoc</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{\mathrm{assoc}}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{j}(x;t)\;=\;\langle C_{t}[j],\phi(x)\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{j}(x;t)\;=\;\langle C_{t}[j],\phi(x)\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Addressing, Gates, and Value Signal</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From the updated HiPPO state <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, lightweight networks produce:
(i) a write address <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{key}}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>key</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{key}}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and (ii) a read address <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{query}}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>query</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{query}}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, both via learned linear projections with a sigmoid activations, and
(iii) a write gate <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\text{write}}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>write</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\text{write}}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and (iv) an output gate <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\text{out}}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\text{out}}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, both via two-layer MLPs with hidden width 256, tanh hidden activations, a residual connection, and sigmoid output activations.
The value signal written to memory is a learned linear map of the channel input, <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Minimum-norm Write Update (Orthogonal Projection)</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{t}=\phi(x_{\text{key}})\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{\text{assoc}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>key</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>assoc</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{t}=\phi(x_{\text{key}})\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{\text{assoc}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and define the current memory evaluation (per channel)</p>
<table id="A3.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{y}_{t}[j]\;=\;\langle C_{t}[j],k_{t}\rangle,\qquad j=1,\dots,d_{\text{model}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{t}[j]\;=\;\langle C_{t}[j],k_{t}\rangle,\qquad j=1,\dots,d_{\text{model}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We update <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to move <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> toward the desired value <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> while changing coefficients as little as possible in the <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm. Specifically, for each channel <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> we solve</p>
<table id="A3.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta C_{t}[j]\;=\;\arg\min_{\Delta\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{\text{assoc}}}}\|\Delta\|_{2}^{2}\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\langle C_{t}[j]+\Delta,k_{t}\rangle=(1-g_{\text{write}})\,\hat{y}_{t}[j]+g_{\text{write}}\,y_{t}[j]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>assoc</mtext></msub></msup></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>write</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>write</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta C_{t}[j]\;=\;\arg\min_{\Delta\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{\text{assoc}}}}\|\Delta\|_{2}^{2}\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\langle C_{t}[j]+\Delta,k_{t}\rangle=(1-g_{\text{write}})\,\hat{y}_{t}[j]+g_{\text{write}}\,y_{t}[j].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This constraint interpolates between no write (<math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\text{write}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>write</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\text{write}}=0</annotation></semantics></math>) and a full write (<math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\text{write}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>write</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\text{write}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>). The solution follows from a Lagrange multiplier and yields the closed-form update</p>
<table id="A3.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t+1}[j]\;=\;C_{t}[j]+\alpha_{t}\,\big(y_{t}[j]-\hat{y}_{t}[j]\big)\,k_{t},\qquad\alpha_{t}\;=\;\frac{g_{\text{write}}}{\|k_{t}\|_{2}^{2}+\varepsilon}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>write</mtext></msub><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t+1}[j]\;=\;C_{t}[j]+\alpha_{t}\,\big(y_{t}[j]-\hat{y}_{t}[j]\big)\,k_{t},\qquad\alpha_{t}\;=\;\frac{g_{\text{write}}}{\|k_{t}\|_{2}^{2}+\varepsilon}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Because the basis is orthonormal, <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|k_{t}\|_{2}^{2}=\langle k_{t},k_{t}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|k_{t}\|_{2}^{2}=\langle k_{t},k_{t}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is well-conditioned. Interpreted in function space, Eq. (<a href="#A3.Ex24" title="Minimum-norm Write Update (Orthogonal Projection) ‣ C.3 Associative Recall Experiment ‣ Appendix C Experimental Details ‣ HiPPO Zoo: Explicit Memory Mechanisms for Interpretable State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>) adds a localized bump proportional to the truncated reproducing kernel <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(x_{\text{key}},x)=\phi(x_{\text{key}})^{\top}\phi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>key</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>key</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(x_{\text{key}},x)=\phi(x_{\text{key}})^{\top}\phi(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, making interference between different addresses explicit.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reading and Output</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reading evaluates the updated memory at the query address:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}[j]\;=\;\langle C_{t+1}[j],q_{t}\rangle,\qquad q_{t}=\phi(x_{\text{query}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>query</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}[j]\;=\;\langle C_{t+1}[j],q_{t}\rangle,\qquad q_{t}=\phi(x_{\text{query}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">forming a retrieved vector <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. A linear output projection maps to token space and is gated:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{t}\;=\;g_{\text{out}}\,W_{\text{out}}r_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{t}\;=\;g_{\text{out}}\,W_{\text{out}}r_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training Protocol</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training proceeds online: each iteration samples one episode, initializes <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S_{0},C_{0})=(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S_{0},C_{0})=(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, and rolls out the dynamics sequentially. We use truncated backpropagation through time (TBPTT) with chunk length equal to the episode length in our experiments, applying a stop-gradient operation to <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,C)</annotation></semantics></math> at chunk boundaries. Parameters are optimized with AdamW with learning rate <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> to minimize mean squared error between <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the target output at the <span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Write</span> timestep and zero elsewhere.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.4 </span>Variable Horizon Reconstruction Experiment</h3>

<section id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data</h4>

<div id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We generate a one-dimensional stationary signal <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{0:T-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0:T-1}</annotation></semantics></math> as an equal-weight mixture of <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> independent Ornstein–Uhlenbeck (OU) components with log-uniform time constants. Concretely, for <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t=1</annotation></semantics></math> and component timescales <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}\sim\mathrm{LogUniform}(2,2000)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>LogUniform</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2000</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}\sim\mathrm{LogUniform}(2,2000)</annotation></semantics></math>, each component evolves as an AR(1)</p>
<table id="A3.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="z^{(k)}_{t+1}=a_{k}z^{(k)}_{t}+b_{k}\varepsilon^{(k)}_{t},\qquad a_{k}=\exp(-\Delta t/\tau_{k}),\qquad b_{k}=\sqrt{1-a_{k}^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{(k)}_{t+1}=a_{k}z^{(k)}_{t}+b_{k}\varepsilon^{(k)}_{t},\qquad a_{k}=\exp(-\Delta t/\tau_{k}),\qquad b_{k}=\sqrt{1-a_{k}^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon^{(k)}_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon^{(k)}_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{(k)}_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{(k)}_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. The observed signal is</p>
<table id="A3.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{K}}\sum_{k=1}^{K}z^{(k)}_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>K</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{K}}\sum_{k=1}^{K}z^{(k)}_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">which has unit stationary variance. We report results averaged over 64 independently sampled trajectories of length <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=3\times 10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=3\times 10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multiscale and Single Scale Systems</h4>

<div id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All systems are run online on the full sequence to produce a final state at time <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-1</annotation></semantics></math>. We compare:
(i) three Multiscale HiPPO variants (basic <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial, Jeffreys-weighted <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial, and log-timescale <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=\log g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=\log g</annotation></semantics></math>; see Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Multiscale HiPPO: Additional Derivations and Details ‣ HiPPO Zoo: Explicit Memory Mechanisms for Interpretable State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> for complete descriptions), each with HiPPO order <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=16</annotation></semantics></math> and scale dimension <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=128</annotation></semantics></math>, so the total state has dimension <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nm</annotation></semantics></math>; and
(ii) a bank of vanilla HiPPO systems at fixed base timescales <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\{10,100,1000,10000\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>10,100</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1000</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10000</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\{10,100,1000,10000\}</annotation></semantics></math>, each storing <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=16</annotation></semantics></math> coefficients.
All systems use the same underlying continuous-time HiPPO Leg-T measure and are discretized with a zero-order hold (ZOH) step of <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the multiscale systems, we implement the coupled linear dynamics</p>
<table id="A3.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{S}(t)=A\,S(t)\,M+B\,x(t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{S}(t)=A\,S(t)\,M+B\,x(t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(t)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(t)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}</annotation></semantics></math> is the multiscale state, <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the HiPPO generator (scaled by a base timescale parameter), and <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times m}</annotation></semantics></math> is the scale-coupling matrix (tridiagonal for polynomial-in-<math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> variants; dense for the <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=\log g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=\log g</annotation></semantics></math> variant). In code we vectorize <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and discretize the resulting linear system exactly using ZOH to obtain a discrete-time update</p>
<table id="A3.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{t+1}=A_{d}\mathbf{s}_{t}+B_{d}x_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{t+1}=A_{d}\mathbf{s}_{t}+B_{d}x_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{t}=\mathrm{vec}(S(t))\in\mathbb{R}^{nm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{t}=\mathrm{vec}(S(t))\in\mathbb{R}^{nm}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Querying a Target Horizon</h4>

<div id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate reconstruction at horizon <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in\{L_{1},\dots,L_{R}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in\{L_{1},\dots,L_{R}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we interpret each system’s output as <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=16</annotation></semantics></math> orthonormal shifted Legendre coefficients on a normalized coordinate <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> spanning the requested window. We fix the convention</p>
<table id="A3.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0\ \leftrightarrow\ \text{present time }(T-1),\qquad s=1\ \leftrightarrow\ \text{past endpoint }(T-1-L),\qquad t(s)=(T-1)-sL." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em" stretchy="false">↔</mo><mrow><mtext>present time </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em" stretchy="false">↔</mo><mrow><mtext>past endpoint </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0\ \leftrightarrow\ \text{present time }(T-1),\qquad s=1\ \leftrightarrow\ \text{past endpoint }(T-1-L),\qquad t(s)=(T-1)-sL.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Given coefficients <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(L)\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(L)\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> in the orthonormal shifted Legendre basis <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\tilde{P}_{k}(s)\}_{k=0}^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\tilde{P}_{k}(s)\}_{k=0}^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, the reconstructed curve is</p>
<table id="A3.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{L}(s)=\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}c_{k}(L)\,\tilde{P}_{k}(s)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{L}(s)=\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}c_{k}(L)\,\tilde{P}_{k}(s).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> on a uniform grid of <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=128</annotation></semantics></math> points in <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Multiscale Systems</em>
For each horizon <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, the multiscale state is queried by evaluating the scale-basis vector at an inverse-horizon parameter and contracting along the scale dimension:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(L)=S(T-1)\,q(L)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(L)=S(T-1)\,q(L),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(L)\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(L)\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is the scale-basis evaluated at the appropriate scale. In the basic <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomial construction, we use</p>
<table id="A3.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(L)=\mathrm{clip}\!\left(\frac{\tau_{0}}{L},\,0,\,1\right),\qquad q(L)=\boldsymbol{\ell}\big(g(L)\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.428em;" width="1.428em"><mi>clip</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn> 0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(L)=\mathrm{clip}\!\left(\frac{\tau_{0}}{L},\,0,\,1\right),\qquad q(L)=\boldsymbol{\ell}\big(g(L)\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> the multiscale base timescale used to scale <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> the orthonormal shifted Legendre basis on <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the Jeffreys-weighted <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> variant, the basis is replaced by orthonormal polynomials for <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(g)\propto 1/g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(g)\propto 1/g</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\epsilon,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\epsilon,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and we clip <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(L)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\epsilon,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\epsilon,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the log-timescale <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=\log g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=\log g</annotation></semantics></math> variant, we set</p>
<table id="A3.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(L)=\mathrm{clip}\!\left(\frac{\tau_{0}}{L},\,\epsilon,\,1\right),\qquad u(L)=\log g(L)\in[\log\epsilon,0],\qquad q(L)=\boldsymbol{\psi}\big(u(L)\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.428em;" width="1.428em"><mi>clip</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝝍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(L)=\mathrm{clip}\!\left(\frac{\tau_{0}}{L},\,\epsilon,\,1\right),\qquad u(L)=\log g(L)\in[\log\epsilon,0],\qquad q(L)=\boldsymbol{\psi}\big(u(L)\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> is an orthonormal polynomial basis that is uniform in <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Vanilla HiPPO Systems</em>
Each fixed-timescale HiPPO system with base window <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> produces coefficients <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{(\tau)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{(\tau)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> that summarize the past over a fixed window of length <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, with its own normalized coordinate <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\tau}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\tau}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> defined by</p>
<table id="A3.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t(s_{\tau})=(T-1)-s_{\tau}\tau." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t(s_{\tau})=(T-1)-s_{\tau}\tau.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">To compare fairly at requested horizon <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, we evaluate the vanilla system on the <em id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">same absolute-time grid</em> <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t(s)=(T-1)-sL" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t(s)=(T-1)-sL</annotation></semantics></math> used above, but map each query time into the system’s coordinate</p>
<table id="A3.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\tau}(t)=\frac{(T-1)-t}{\tau}=\frac{sL}{\tau}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\tau}(t)=\frac{(T-1)-t}{\tau}=\frac{sL}{\tau},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">and set predictions to zero when <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\tau}(t)\notin[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\tau}(t)\notin[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., when the requested time lies outside the system’s representable window). This implements the behavior that fixed-horizon HiPPO cannot represent content earlier than its intrinsic window.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reconstruction error.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each horizon <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the mean squared error (MSE) by comparing <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{L}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{L}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> to the true signal sampled at the corresponding (possibly fractional) times <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t(s)</annotation></semantics></math> via linear interpolation:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MSE}(L)=\frac{1}{S}\sum_{i=1}^{S}\Big(\hat{x}_{L}(s_{i})-x\big(t(s_{i})\big)\Big)^{2},\qquad s_{i}=\frac{i-1}{R-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>MSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>S</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MSE}(L)=\frac{1}{S}\sum_{i=1}^{S}\Big(\hat{x}_{L}(s_{i})-x\big(t(s_{i})\big)\Big)^{2},\qquad s_{i}=\frac{i-1}{R-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MSE}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MSE}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> for each trajectory and report the Monte Carlo mean across trajectories; shaded bands in the plots correspond to the standard error of the mean across the 64 trials.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reported Curves and Example Reconstructions</h4>

<div id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The top panel of Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Multiscale State Representation ‣ 3.4 Multiscale HiPPO ‣ 3 Methods and Results ‣ HiPPO Zoo: Explicit Memory Mechanisms for Interpretable State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> plots average <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MSE}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MSE}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> versus <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> (logarithmic <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-axis) for the log-timescale Multiscale HiPPO system and each fixed-timescale vanilla HiPPO baseline. The bottom panel shows representative reconstructions <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> for several horizons <math id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> on a single example trajectory, illustrating that the multiscale representation yields stable reconstructions over a wide range of horizons under a fixed HiPPO coefficient budget.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.5 </span>Objective-induced Predictive Memory Experiment</h3>

<div id="A3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide additional details for the Forecasting HiPPO experiment in Section <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5 Forecasting HiPPO ‣ 3 Methods and Results ‣ HiPPO Zoo: Explicit Memory Mechanisms for Interpretable State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>, including the system construction, training procedure, and evaluation protocol.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Signal Model</h4>

<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The input signal <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is drawn from a stationary zero-mean Gaussian process constructed as a mixture of independent RBF kernels with different lengthscales,</p>
<table id="A3.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(\Delta)=\sum_{m=1}^{M}w_{m}^{2}\exp\!\left(-\frac{\Delta^{2}}{2\ell_{m}^{2}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(\Delta)=\sum_{m=1}^{M}w_{m}^{2}\exp\!\left(-\frac{\Delta^{2}}{2\ell_{m}^{2}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the weights <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{w_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{w_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are normalized so that <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{m}w_{m}^{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>m</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{m}w_{m}^{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}[x(t)]\approx 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}[x(t)]\approx 1</annotation></semantics></math>. The experimental lengthscales are <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(10^{-1},\ 3,\ 16,\ 32,\ 64)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mn> 3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 16</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 32</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 64</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(10^{-1},\ 3,\ 16,\ 32,\ 64)</annotation></semantics></math> while the corresponding weights are <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\frac{1}{2},\ 2,\ 5,\ 5,\ 5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn> 2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\frac{1}{2},\ 2,\ 5,\ 5,\ 5)</annotation></semantics></math>. Samples are generated on an integer grid using a circulant embedding and FFT-based approximation. The same realization of <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is used for both short- and long-horizon forecasters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">HiPPO Systems and Discretization</h4>

<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each forecaster maintains three continuous-time Leg-T HiPPO systems with <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=64</annotation></semantics></math> coefficients, discretized with an exact zero-order hold (ZOH).</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">System 1 (Recent Past)</span> encodes the signal over a horizon <math id="A3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> using a Legendre HiPPO system on the interval <math id="A3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t-H,t]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t-H,t]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">System 2 (Past-before-Past)</span> integrates a scalar lagged signal extracted from System 1, approximating <math id="A3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t-H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t-H)</annotation></semantics></math>, and therefore represents the interval preceding the recent window.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">System 3 (Aligned Past)</span> has identical dynamics to System 2, but is driven directly by the true input <math id="A3.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, allowing it to encode the recent window in the same coordinate system as System 2.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Systems 2 and 3 have timescale of <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math>, while System 1 has a timescale of <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=4</annotation></semantics></math> for the short horizon forecaster, and <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=32</annotation></semantics></math> for the long horizon forecaster.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Learning the Forecast Map</h4>

<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Forecasting is posed as a linear prediction problem between HiPPO states. Let</p>
<table id="A3.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}=S_{2}(t),\qquad y_{t}=S_{1}(t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}=S_{2}(t),\qquad y_{t}=S_{1}(t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> represents history preceding the forecast window and <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the recent window state to be predicted.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During a single online pass over the training signal of length <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=2\times 10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=2\times 10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, we accumulate second-order statistics</p>
<table id="A3.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{xx}=\mathbb{E}[x_{t}x_{t}^{\top}],\quad\Sigma_{yy}=\mathbb{E}[y_{t}y_{t}^{\top}],\quad\Sigma_{yx}=\mathbb{E}[y_{t}x_{t}^{\top}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{xx}=\mathbb{E}[x_{t}x_{t}^{\top}],\quad\Sigma_{yy}=\mathbb{E}[y_{t}y_{t}^{\top}],\quad\Sigma_{yx}=\mathbb{E}[y_{t}x_{t}^{\top}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">using simple running averages.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We then compute a rank-<math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> reduced-rank regression (RRR) predictor</p>
<table id="A3.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{t}=T_{d}x_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{t}=T_{d}x_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> minimizes <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\|y_{t}-Tx_{t}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\|y_{t}-Tx_{t}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> subject to <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{rank}(T)\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{rank}(T)\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>. This is obtained via an SVD of the whitened cross-covariance, as detailed in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Forecast-induced history geometry and the identity-metric case ‣ HiPPO Zoo: Explicit Memory Mechanisms for Interpretable State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>. The same procedure is applied independently for short- and long-horizon forecasters, which differ only in the System 1 horizon <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Predictive Memory and Projection</h4>

<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the forecast map <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the RRR solution yields a rank-<math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> projector</p>
<table id="A3.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{x}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{n}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{x}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which projects System 3 states onto the subspace of history representations that are maximally predictive of the future over the chosen horizon. We refer to <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{x}S_{3}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{x}S_{3}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> as the <em id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">predictive memory</em> associated with history <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Both the full System 3 state and its predictive projection are decoded back into function space using the standard HiPPO evaluation map, allowing direct visualization of which portions of the past are retained or discarded by the forecasting objective.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As a reference for the HiPPO forecasts, we additionally compute the Gaussian process posterior mean and uncertainty conditioned on the same observed history. All plots use a zero-order-hold (piecewise constant) convention consistent with the discretized HiPPO dynamics.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To characterize the geometry induced by the forecasting objective, we compute the operator</p>
<table id="A3.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=T^{\top}T," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=T^{\top}T,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and map it back to the time domain by evaluating the orthogonal polynomial basis on a dense lag grid, yielding a history-space kernel whose leading eigenfunctions are shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Variable-Horizon Reconstruction Experiment ‣ 3.4 Multiscale HiPPO ‣ 3 Methods and Results ‣ HiPPO Zoo: Explicit Memory Mechanisms for Interpretable State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Multiscale HiPPO: Additional Derivations and Details</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix provides additional technical details for Multiscale HiPPO that are omitted from the main text. We first derive the basic multiscale construction using polynomial embeddings of the inverse timescale parameter <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. We then discuss Jeffreys-style scale embeddings and explain why they do not alter the limiting spectral properties of the resulting system. Finally, we give further details for the log-timescale formulation <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=\log g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=\log g</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the variant used in the main text experiments. Figure <a href="#A4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ D.2 Jeffreys-Style Scale Embeddings ‣ Appendix D Multiscale HiPPO: Additional Derivations and Details ‣ HiPPO Zoo: Explicit Memory Mechanisms for Interpretable State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> compares the three methods on the variable-horizon reconstruction experiment in Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Multiscale HiPPO ‣ 3 Methods and Results ‣ HiPPO Zoo: Explicit Memory Mechanisms for Interpretable State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Basic Multiscale HiPPO via Polynomial Embeddings in <math id="A4.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that for a fixed inverse timescale <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the continuous-time HiPPO dynamics are</p>
<table id="A4.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\mathbf{s}}(t;g)=g\big[A\,\mathbf{s}(t;g)+\mathbf{b}\,f(t)\big]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\mathbf{s}}(t;g)=g\big[A\,\mathbf{s}(t;g)+\mathbf{b}\,f(t)\big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}(t;g)\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}(t;g)\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> are the HiPPO coefficients at scale <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. To represent a continuum of such states, we assume that <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}(t;g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}(t;g)</annotation></semantics></math> admits an expansion in an orthonormal polynomial basis on <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A4.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}(t;g)\;\approx\;S(t)\,\boldsymbol{\ell}(g),\qquad\boldsymbol{\ell}(g)=\big(L_{0}(g),\dots,L_{M-1}(g)\big)^{\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}(t;g)\;\approx\;S(t)\,\boldsymbol{\ell}(g),\qquad\boldsymbol{\ell}(g)=\big(L_{0}(g),\dots,L_{M-1}(g)\big)^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{L_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{L_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are shifted Legendre polynomials orthonormal on <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(t)\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(t)\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times M}</annotation></semantics></math> collects the scale coefficients.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Substituting this ansatz into the HiPPO dynamics gives</p>
<table id="A4.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{S}(t)\,\boldsymbol{\ell}(g)=gAS(t)\,\boldsymbol{\ell}(g)+g\mathbf{b}\,f(t)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐛</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{S}(t)\,\boldsymbol{\ell}(g)=gAS(t)\,\boldsymbol{\ell}(g)+g\mathbf{b}\,f(t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Multiplication by <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> acts linearly on the polynomial basis, so there exists a matrix <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times M}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="A4.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\,\boldsymbol{\ell}(g)=C\,\boldsymbol{\ell}(g)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\,\boldsymbol{\ell}(g)=C\,\boldsymbol{\ell}(g).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">For orthonormal polynomials on a compact interval, Favard’s theorem guarantees that <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a symmetric tridiagonal matrix (see Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Orthogonal Polynomial Background ‣ HiPPO Zoo: Explicit Memory Mechanisms for Interpretable State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for a statement of Favard’s theorem). Choosing</p>
<table id="A4.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times M}\quad\text{such that}\quad B\,\boldsymbol{\ell}(g)=g\,\mathbf{b}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>such that</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐛</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times M}\quad\text{such that}\quad B\,\boldsymbol{\ell}(g)=g\,\mathbf{b},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">we obtain the multiscale dynamics</p>
<table id="A4.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{S}=ASC+Bf(t)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{S}=ASC+Bf(t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Vectorizing the state yields</p>
<table id="A4.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{vec}(\dot{S})=(C^{\top}\otimes A)\,\mathrm{vec}(S)+\mathrm{vec}(B)\,f(t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>vec</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>vec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{vec}(\dot{S})=(C^{\top}\otimes A)\,\mathrm{vec}(S)+\mathrm{vec}(B)\,f(t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.6" class="ltx_p">and since <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is symmetric, its eigenvalues <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\lambda_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\lambda_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> lie in <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. The spectrum of the full system consists of products <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{m}\mu_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{m}\mu_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{n}\in\mathrm{eig}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>eig</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{n}\in\mathrm{eig}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to a bank of HiPPO systems at effective timescales indexed by <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Jeffreys-Style Scale Embeddings</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A natural idea is to bias the multiscale representation toward long horizons by replacing the uniform measure on <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> with a Jeffreys-style prior <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(g)\propto 1/g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(g)\propto 1/g</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\epsilon,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\epsilon,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. One can construct an orthonormal polynomial basis <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\tilde{L}_{m}(g)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\tilde{L}_{m}(g)\}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to this weight and repeat the derivation above. Multiplication by <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is again represented by a symmetric tridiagonal Jacobi matrix <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding dynamics of the same form as above.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, the spectral consequences are subtler than a naive “prior over scales” interpretation might suggest. For any sufficiently regular positive weight on a compact interval, the empirical eigenvalue distribution of the associated Jacobi matrix converges, as <math id="A4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, to a universal arcsine law on that interval. Specifically, Proposition 1.2 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kuijlaars and Van Assche, 1999</a>)</cite> states that for a measure <math id="A4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> supported on a single interval <math id="A4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\alpha,\beta]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha,\beta]</annotation></semantics></math> with positive density almost everywhere, the normalized zero counting measure of the degree-<math id="A4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> orthogonal polynomial converges weakly to the equilibrium (arcsine) measure on <math id="A4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\alpha,\beta]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha,\beta]</annotation></semantics></math>, with density</p>
<table id="A4.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{\pi\sqrt{(\beta-g)(g-\alpha)}}\,\mathbf{1}_{(\alpha,\beta)}(g)\,dg." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold"> 1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{\pi\sqrt{(\beta-g)(g-\alpha)}}\,\mathbf{1}_{(\alpha,\beta)}(g)\,dg.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Since the eigenvalues of the <math id="A4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times M</annotation></semantics></math> Jacobi truncation coincide with the zeros of the degree-<math id="A4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> orthogonal polynomial, the empirical eigenvalue distribution of the Jacobi matrix converges to the same arcsine law. Consequently, reweighting the measure on <math id="A4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> (while preserving compact single-interval support and positivity of the density) does not change the limiting distribution of eigenvalues, and therefore does not fundamentally alter the characteristic timescales represented by the system. This motivates the alternative log-timescale construction described next.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21340v1/figs/multiscale_comparison.png" id="A4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:357/134;" width="357" height="134" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="A4.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The log-timescale Multiscale HiPPO variant consistently outperforms the basic and Jeffreys-style variants on the variable-horizon reconstruction experiment from Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Multiscale HiPPO ‣ 3 Methods and Results ‣ HiPPO Zoo: Explicit Memory Mechanisms for Interpretable State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. Single-scale HiPPO systems with timescales <math id="A4.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{1},\dots,10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{1},\dots,10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> are shown in light to dark blue for comparison. Shaded regions denote <math id="A4.F6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> SEM for 64 independently sampled trajectories of length <math id="A4.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=3\times 10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=3\times 10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Log-Timescale Formulation (<math id="A4.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=\log g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=\log g</annotation></semantics></math>)</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To obtain an embedding that allocates resolution uniformly across decades of timescale, we instead parameterize</p>
<table id="A4.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\triangleq\log g\in[\log\epsilon,0]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\triangleq\log g\in[\log\epsilon,0],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and represent the scale-dependent state as a function of <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}(t;u)\;\approx\;S(t)\,\boldsymbol{\psi}(u)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝝍</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}(t;u)\;\approx\;S(t)\,\boldsymbol{\psi}(u),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\psi_{m}(u)\}_{m=0}^{M-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\psi_{m}(u)\}_{m=0}^{M-1}</annotation></semantics></math> are orthonormal polynomials on <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\log\epsilon,0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\log\epsilon,0]</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the uniform measure.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The underlying HiPPO dynamics become</p>
<table id="A4.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\mathbf{s}}(t;u)=e^{u}\big[A\,\mathbf{s}(t;u)+\mathbf{b}\,f(t)\big]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\mathbf{s}}(t;u)=e^{u}\big[A\,\mathbf{s}(t;u)+\mathbf{b}\,f(t)\big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In the <math id="A4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\psi_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\psi_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> basis, multiplication by <math id="A4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a symmetric tridiagonal Jacobi matrix <math id="A4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>, while multiplication by <math id="A4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(u)=e^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(u)=e^{u}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by the matrix function</p>
<table id="A4.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\exp(J)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\exp(J).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Substituting the expansion yields multiscale dynamics</p>
<table id="A4.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{S}=ASG+Bf(t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{S}=ASG+Bf(t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">with an analogous rank-<math id="A4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> input injection <math id="A4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\mathbf{b}\mathbf{r}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐛𝐫</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\mathbf{b}\mathbf{r}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> are the <math id="A4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\psi_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\psi_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math>-coefficients of <math id="A4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{u}</annotation></semantics></math> (equivalently, <math id="A4.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}=G\mathbf{e}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}=G\mathbf{e}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>). Unlike the basic construction, the matrix <math id="A4.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is generally dense, and the <math id="A4.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(NM)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(NM)</annotation></semantics></math> update cost associated with a tridiagonal scale-coupling matrix is lost. Nevertheless, for moderate <math id="A4.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> can be precomputed explicitly, or applied implicitly as a matrix function of <math id="A4.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> using Krylov or polynomial approximation methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The advantage of the <math id="A4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=\log g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=\log g</annotation></semantics></math> formulation is conceptual and practical: the spectrum of <math id="A4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> distributes over <math id="A4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\log\epsilon,0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\log\epsilon,0]</annotation></semantics></math>, and the spectrum of <math id="A4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\exp(J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\exp(J)</annotation></semantics></math> is its exponential pushforward onto <math id="A4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\epsilon,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\epsilon,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. This yields a representation whose effective timescales are approximately log-uniformly distributed, allowing substantially more resolution at long horizons for a fixed scale dimension <math id="A4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the experiments reported in the main text, we use this log-timescale formulation, as it provides the most stable coverage of a wide range of temporal horizons under a fixed coefficient budget. See Figure <a href="#A4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ D.2 Jeffreys-Style Scale Embeddings ‣ Appendix D Multiscale HiPPO: Additional Derivations and Details ‣ HiPPO Zoo: Explicit Memory Mechanisms for Interpretable State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> for an empirical comparison between the three multiscale systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.4 </span>Relation to Banks of HiPPO Systems</h3>

<div id="A4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although the multiscale dynamics are written in a coupled form, they do not introduce fundamentally richer dynamics than a bank of independent HiPPO systems. In particular, the scale-coupling operator <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> admits a spectral decomposition, under which the system decouples into a set of HiPPO systems operating at fixed effective timescales, up to a linear rotation of the input injection <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. The advantage of Multiscale HiPPO is therefore not increased expressivity, but a structured and queryable parameterization that compresses a continuum of timescales into a single state with shared structure and controlled complexity.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Forecast-induced history geometry and the identity-metric case</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix justifies the coefficient-space approximation <math id="A5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\propto T^{\top}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\propto T^{\top}T</annotation></semantics></math> used in the main text (Section <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5 Forecasting HiPPO ‣ 3 Methods and Results ‣ HiPPO Zoo: Explicit Memory Mechanisms for Interpretable State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>) and in Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Variable-Horizon Reconstruction Experiment ‣ 3.4 Multiscale HiPPO ‣ 3 Methods and Results ‣ HiPPO Zoo: Explicit Memory Mechanisms for Interpretable State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Throughout, let <math id="A5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> denote a history (the restriction of the signal to <math id="A5.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(-\infty,t]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-\infty,t]</annotation></semantics></math>). Let <math id="A5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(h)\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(h)\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite-dimensional encoding of <math id="A5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> (in our experiments, <math id="A5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(h)</annotation></semantics></math> is the HiPPO coefficient state of System 2/3), and let <math id="A5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y(h)\in\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y(h)\in\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> be the target representation of the future over a horizon <math id="A5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> (in our experiments, <math id="A5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y(h)</annotation></semantics></math> is the System 1 Legendre coefficient vector for the window <math id="A5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t,t+H]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t,t+H]</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">General weighted forecasting losses and the induced bilinear form.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a forecasting objective defined by a weighted squared error over the horizon:</p>
<table id="A5.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\;=\;\mathbb{E}\!\left[\int_{0}^{H}w(\tau)\,\big(\hat{f}_{h}(\tau)-f(t+\tau)\big)^{2}\,\mathrm{d}\tau\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\;=\;\mathbb{E}\!\left[\int_{0}^{H}w(\tau)\,\big(\hat{f}_{h}(\tau)-f(t+\tau)\big)^{2}\,\mathrm{d}\tau\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\tau)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\tau)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> is a weighting function. Suppose <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}_{h}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}_{h}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> is represented in a basis <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{k}(\tau)\}_{k=1}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{k}(\tau)\}_{k=1}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> via a coefficient vector <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}(h)\in\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}(h)\in\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A5.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}_{h}(\tau)\;=\;\sum_{k=1}^{p}\hat{y}_{k}(h)\,\phi_{k}(\tau)\qquad\text{and similarly}\qquad f(t+\tau)\approx\sum_{k=1}^{p}y_{k}(h)\,\phi_{k}(\tau)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and similarly</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≈</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}_{h}(\tau)\;=\;\sum_{k=1}^{p}\hat{y}_{k}(h)\,\phi_{k}(\tau)\qquad\text{and similarly}\qquad f(t+\tau)\approx\sum_{k=1}^{p}y_{k}(h)\,\phi_{k}(\tau).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Then the weighted <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> loss is a quadratic form in coefficient space,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{0}^{H}w(\tau)\,\big(\hat{f}_{h}(\tau)-f(t+\tau)\big)^{2}\,\mathrm{d}\tau\;=\;\big(\hat{y}(h)-y(h)\big)^{\top}W\,\big(\hat{y}(h)-y(h)\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{0}^{H}w(\tau)\,\big(\hat{f}_{h}(\tau)-f(t+\tau)\big)^{2}\,\mathrm{d}\tau\;=\;\big(\hat{y}(h)-y(h)\big)^{\top}W\,\big(\hat{y}(h)-y(h)\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where the metric <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathbb{R}^{p\times p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathbb{R}^{p\times p}</annotation></semantics></math> is the weighted Gram matrix</p>
<table id="A5.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{ij}\;\triangleq\;\int_{0}^{H}w(\tau)\,\phi_{i}(\tau)\,\phi_{j}(\tau)\,\mathrm{d}\tau." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.391em">≜</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{ij}\;\triangleq\;\int_{0}^{H}w(\tau)\,\phi_{i}(\tau)\,\phi_{j}(\tau)\,\mathrm{d}\tau.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">This immediately defines a positive semidefinite bilinear form on histories through predicted futures:</p>
<table id="A5.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle h,h^{\prime}\rangle\;\triangleq\;\mathbb{E}\!\left[\hat{y}(h)^{\top}W\,\hat{y}(h^{\prime})\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≜</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle h,h^{\prime}\rangle\;\triangleq\;\mathbb{E}\!\left[\hat{y}(h)^{\top}W\,\hat{y}(h^{\prime})\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Linear predictors induce <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=T^{\top}WT" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=T^{\top}WT</annotation></semantics></math>.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the linear Forecasting HiPPO setting, the predicted future coefficients are linear in the history state:</p>
<table id="A5.Ex63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}(h)\;=\;T\,x(h)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}(h)\;=\;T\,x(h),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for some matrix <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathbb{R}^{p\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathbb{R}^{p\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Substituting yields</p>
<table id="A5.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}(h)^{\top}W\,\hat{y}(h^{\prime})\;=\;x(h)^{\top}\big(T^{\top}WT\big)\,x(h^{\prime})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}(h)^{\top}W\,\hat{y}(h^{\prime})\;=\;x(h)^{\top}\big(T^{\top}WT\big)\,x(h^{\prime}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Thus the forecasting objective induces a quadratic form on history states,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\;\triangleq\;T^{\top}WT," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≜</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\;\triangleq\;T^{\top}WT,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">which measures how similarly two histories support forecasts under the chosen horizon and weighting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Why <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=I</annotation></semantics></math> in our experiment (and hence <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=T^{\top}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=T^{\top}T</annotation></semantics></math>).</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, the target <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y(h)</annotation></semantics></math> is the vector of Legendre HiPPO coefficients produced by System 1, and we train <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> using a squared Euclidean loss in this coefficient space:</p>
<table id="A5.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\!\left[\|y(h)-Tx(h)\|_{2}^{2}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\!\left[\|y(h)-Tx(h)\|_{2}^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Because the System 1 coefficients correspond to an <em id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">orthonormal</em> Legendre basis on <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> under the (uniform) measure used by the HiPPO reconstruction, the natural coefficient-space metric is the identity:</p>
<table id="A5.Ex67" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=I." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=I.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Therefore the induced history quadratic form simplifies to</p>
<table id="A5.Ex68" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=T^{\top}T." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=T^{\top}T.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">(If one instead measured error in the time domain with a nonuniform <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>, or used a non-orthonormal future representation, then <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> would be nontrivial and the appropriate form would be <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=T^{\top}WT" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=T^{\top}WT</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Connection to the estimators used in the experiment.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We estimate <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> from streaming second-order statistics of the pair</p>
<table id="A5.Ex69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">(<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=S_{2}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=S_{2}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=S_{1}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=S_{1}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>). The full-rank least-squares map is</p>
<table id="A5.Ex70" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{LS}}=\Sigma_{yx}\,\Sigma_{xx}^{-1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>LS</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{LS}}=\Sigma_{yx}\,\Sigma_{xx}^{-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and the rank-<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> reduced-rank regression (RRR) map <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by truncating the singular directions of the whitened cross-covariance. Either choice yields a valid induced form <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=T^{\top}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=T^{\top}T</annotation></semantics></math> (full-rank or rank-<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>), and in Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Variable-Horizon Reconstruction Experiment ‣ 3.4 Multiscale HiPPO ‣ 3 Methods and Results ‣ HiPPO Zoo: Explicit Memory Mechanisms for Interpretable State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> we visualize this geometry by pushing the rank-<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> back to a lag-domain kernel via basis evaluation on a lag grid.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
